 @ssbVanilla  Primarily Dolphin and PCSX2.
Dolphin is very well optimized at this point so no difference there, but PCSX2 still had heavy slowdown on certain games. The new PC helps with the latter.
The only HD console I've seriously emulated so far is the Wii U.

 @Ebilcake  With worse I mean that is less powerful. DLSS is better than FSR but I don't see myself using either if I pay 800$ for a GPU. Also as I have said earlier, power efficiency and low noise are not really a selling point when Radeon cards don't overheat and run quiet.

Oh and also, I have tried RT in Ratchet & Clank on my RTX 3090 and the only difference I saw was in my FPS. 

Taking all this in consideration, It all comes down to rasterization performance. My personal recommendation would be to get the card with more VRAM and better performance. AI upscalers, frame generation (which btw introduces input lag) and Ray tracing are all cool gimmicks, but not really a selling point as of right now.

 @Koeras16  I see. But you shouldnt need to reinstall drivers after bad OC. Normally all OC settings are reset after a crash (or update). Adrenaline is usually overly diligent with that.

Maybe uninstall drivers with DDU, and reinstall?

Something is definitely going wrong.

 @vuri3798 
That's a good point
Now I'm not so sure about re-reviewing either

 @inmypaants  That logic holds if all the graphs only had the current and previous version, plus maybe the 4070ti. But there's stuff all the way down to the 2060 here!

The whole reason this vid exists is because the price and performance has changed over time. You can't go back to a launch review to compare it to other cards anymore.

It'd be a nice to have, not a requirement, chill.

 @keldon1137  The VR space is still a niche sector in the gaming market by all accounts. 12GB isn't sufficient for VR either if we're really digging into it. 

With how today's games are and how resource heavy AAA games are (particularly unoptimized console ports and UE 5 titles being phased in), you'll be turning down settings 1-2 years into the card's lifecycle, which imo is a terrible feeling. Both cards are comparable. RT is objectively better with the 4070 Ti, but it's not horrible on the 7900 XT. You'll have to use DLSS/FSR either way to keep it useable. As far as power idle issues, it can happen with any card and can usually be fixed with tweaking settings in the software; that's to also say revisions have already been released from what I've read.

You'd honestly have to have more incentive (and hyper specific use cases) outside gaming where GeForce cards are absolutely vital to go forward with Nvidia in the future if they're just going to keep gimping the cards out the gate with higher-than-life prices. I have a 3080 10GB and unless Nvidia can push out a crazy 80 Class card next gen with 20-24GB of GDDR7 (cause G6X runs hot as shit) at a reasonable price, I'll just go for RDNA 4 or maybe Intel if Battlemage is up to snuff. I can live without nvenc/cuda for my task.
